1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
When the sun goes down, it's a whole different world out there.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Mysterious, thrilling, and sometimes very scary.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Light is the key to rounding up thousands of pounds of writhing nocturnal creatures.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Technology helps the military detect the enemy.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Cops catch culprits.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:28,000
And jet pilots see what's ahead when there's blackness beyond the cockpit.

7
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:33,280
And a dark sky provides the perfect backdrop for dazzling spectacles.

8
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:37,920
Now, the secrets of night on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:53,560
Somewhere in the dark on the surface of the Pacific Ocean,

10
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:56,020
fishermen are seeking an elusive prize.

11
00:00:56,021 --> 00:00:59,740
a prize that can be best won at night.

12
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:03,580
Squid in the hundreds of thousands.

13
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,500
The particular species of squid they're after

14
00:01:07,501 --> 00:01:11,200
are scientifically known as loligo opalescens,

15
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,600
commonly called market squid.

16
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,760
They're found along the west coast of North America,

17
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:19,560
from the Gulf of Alaska to Baja California.

18
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:23,020
But the fishery is concentrated in Southern California,

19
00:01:23,021 --> 00:01:24,620
where the squid spawn.

20
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:33,120
For the crew of the Barbara H, this particular day begins about 50 miles north of Los Angeles,

21
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:36,840
at a time when most fishermen are returning with a morning's catch.

22
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:40,840
It's one of dozens of squid boats setting out at the same time,

23
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:45,260
hoping to beat the others in a high-stakes competition waged on the open ocean,

24
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,480
while most of America is asleep.

25
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:52,000
Squid season is during their spawning season,

26
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:54,400
when these nocturnal sea creatures swim closer to the

27
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:57,040
surface and nearer to the coastline.

28
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,180
But their behavior is unpredictable,

29
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:02,080
and they are often impossible to find.

30
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:05,520
After five hours of heading west, the Barbara H is getting

31
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:08,580
close to where squid have appeared recently.

32
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:12,980
We're about 30 miles off the coast of Ventura at the Channel

33
00:02:13,060 --> 00:02:14,720
Islands at Santa Rosa Island right now.

34
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:16,520
We're heading for our light boat.

35
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:20,220
Last night, he saw 5 to 15 tons of squid,

36
00:02:20,221 --> 00:02:23,220
and hopefully tonight he sees a little more.

37
00:02:23,221 --> 00:02:27,020
Light boats can spend days or weeks at sea tracking squid.

38
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:31,900
As the sun sets, they turn on their secret weapon.

39
00:02:31,901 --> 00:02:36,940
The light boat working with a Barbara H is armed with a total of 30,000 watts,

40
00:02:36,941 --> 00:02:40,700
all aimed down into the water to attract squid.

41
00:02:40,701 --> 00:02:44,620
We're not exactly sure why squid are attracted to the light.

42
00:02:44,621 --> 00:02:46,540
There's a couple of speculations.

43
00:02:46,541 --> 00:02:53,100
One is that the light attracts and congregates food sources for the squid, small plankton, and other creatures.

44
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:57,660
The other theory is that the lights reflect off of the surface of the water,

45
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,200
and it looks like a large group of iridescent squid,

46
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,280
and the squid are attracted to what they think are more squid.

47
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:08,460
The sea is rough, and only a few squid are scattered about.

48
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,420
Their current market price is around $700 per ton,

49
00:03:12,421 --> 00:03:16,620
So Captain Hayworth decides to keep searching for a more profitable catch.

50
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:22,380
Tonight, there are about 70 boats in the area looking for squid,

51
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:27,560
all taking part in one of the unique and mysterious activities that happen only at night.

52
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:33,540
Night in the simplest terms, it's just that the Earth has rotated away from the sun,

53
00:03:33,541 --> 00:03:35,060
the sun's below the horizon.

54
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:36,900
But it's really so much more than that.

55
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,500
You're really entering a new reality.

56
00:03:38,501 --> 00:03:40,640
because when you take away the light from the sun,

57
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:42,580
the entire planet responds to that.

58
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:45,180
In fact, all of the life in the biosphere on this planet

59
00:03:45,181 --> 00:03:48,180
has evolved to be conditioned to this cycle of day and night

60
00:03:48,181 --> 00:03:49,540
that happens in 24 hours.

61
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:54,360
Many animals, like bats and wolves,

62
00:03:54,361 --> 00:03:57,380
are at their most active at night,

63
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:01,340
while humans are hardwired to be awake during the day

64
00:04:01,341 --> 00:04:02,560
and to sleep at night.

65
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:07,220
Scientifically speaking, our natural awake sleep pattern

66
00:04:07,221 --> 00:04:09,160
is called our circadian rhythm,

67
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:12,820
and it's partially regulated by the hormone melatonin.

68
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:16,480
We call this the hormone of darkness

69
00:04:16,481 --> 00:04:19,320
because it's suppressed during daylight hours

70
00:04:19,321 --> 00:04:22,020
and is produced during dark hours,

71
00:04:22,021 --> 00:04:25,440
and it's associated with reductions in core body temperature

72
00:04:25,441 --> 00:04:28,500
and reductions in the circadian waking signal,

73
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:32,220
all of which together converge to make us feel sleepy at night.

74
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:36,900
A disruption of our natural rhythms usually has a negative effect.

75
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:38,800
Case in point, jet lag.

76
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:44,720
When this misalignment occurs, then all sorts of problems ensue,

77
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:47,900
such as gastrointestinal problems, headache, drowsiness,

78
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:49,880
just a general feeling of malaise.

79
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:56,080
Sleep research has influenced the design of spacecraft environments.

80
00:04:57,560 --> 00:05:00,480
Enabling astronauts to experience an artificial day and night

81
00:05:00,481 --> 00:05:03,460
and get enough sleep has proven beneficial.

82
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:07,520
But down here on Earth, ever since we've been able to light up the night,

83
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,560
we've gotten into habits that conflict with our natural rhythms.

84
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:17,780
Humans are increasingly engaging in behaviors that attempt to override these internal biological rhythms,

85
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,200
taking stimulants, including coffee, or taking sleep aids at night to help us sleep.

86
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:25,780
But many of us are willing to risk those problems to make a living.

87
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:28,280
We call it working the graveyard shift.

88
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:33,040
And that's what fishermen must do to catch squid.

89
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:35,260
It looks like a good spot of fish here,

90
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:37,300
so I think we're going to try a set.

91
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:40,260
It looks like there should be 20 to 50 tons.

92
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:42,600
So stand by, guys.

93
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,200
We're going to let her go here in a minute.

94
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:45,700
Stand by.

95
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:48,480
As the school of squid gathers under the lights,

96
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:51,520
the crew releases a skip from the back of the fishing boat.

97
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:55,520
It serves to anchor one end of a 1,200-foot-long net,

98
00:05:55,620 --> 00:05:58,580
while the rest of the net deploys off the back of the Barbara H

99
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:01,860
as it circles the light boat.

100
00:06:01,861 --> 00:06:07,860
The lightboat stays in the center to make sure the squid stay put and don't try to escape.

101
00:06:07,861 --> 00:06:13,860
Another way the lightboat captain ensures a good catch is to use small explosives called seal bombs.

102
00:06:13,861 --> 00:06:16,860
These are underwater M80s. They're seal control.

103
00:06:16,861 --> 00:06:20,860
They help keep away the sea lions and the unwanted fish.

104
00:06:20,861 --> 00:06:24,860
But as an added bonus, they're used to bring up squid.

105
00:06:24,861 --> 00:06:27,860
They help tighten up the squid in our net.

106
00:06:27,861 --> 00:06:34,040
When the circle is complete, the skiff hands off the end of the net, and the fishing boat crew begins to haul it in.

107
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,580
The net, hanging like a curtain and often near or touching the ocean floor, is slowly cinched in.

108
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:47,980
First the bottom, then the gap in the side closes as the circumference of the net shrinks.

109
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:52,280
Once the squid are trapped, the light boat moves out of the circle.

110
00:06:52,281 --> 00:06:56,280
Then the crew, aided by powerful winches,

111
00:06:56,281 --> 00:06:59,280
pulls the net in tighter and tighter until it's right up

112
00:06:59,281 --> 00:07:01,280
against the side of the boat.

113
00:07:01,281 --> 00:07:04,280
We sack up the net, which is when we bring the net all the

114
00:07:04,281 --> 00:07:07,280
way up and we bring it up tight to where we have pretty much

115
00:07:07,281 --> 00:07:09,280
solid fish with just a little bit of water in it.

116
00:07:09,281 --> 00:07:12,280
Then we lower down a fish pump, a 10-inch fish pump,

117
00:07:12,281 --> 00:07:14,280
that pumps the fish aboard.

118
00:07:14,281 --> 00:07:18,280
And we can pump about three tons a minute.

119
00:07:18,281 --> 00:07:21,280
By the thousands, the spacecraft

120
00:07:21,281 --> 00:07:24,040
By the thousands, the squid are sucked up through the pump's hose,

121
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:27,320
slide down a ramp, and pour into the boat's hold.

122
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:32,460
This catch turns out to be about 40 tons of squid,

123
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:34,800
but the Barbara H is a big boat,

124
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:37,180
and its hold is still less than half full.

125
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:39,960
Once the squid are aboard,

126
00:07:40,420 --> 00:07:42,340
the crew quickly pulls in the fish pump,

127
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:44,260
cleans up the deck,

128
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:46,460
and resets for another catch.

129
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:49,120
Generally, when we're fishing squid,

130
00:07:49,121 --> 00:07:55,720
We fish from sunset to sunrise, and it makes for a long night.

131
00:07:55,820 --> 00:07:57,760
By the end of this night, the Barbara H.

132
00:07:57,860 --> 00:07:59,900
has 55 tons of squid on board.

133
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:01,960
Most will end up on dinner plates,

134
00:08:02,060 --> 00:08:05,340
while some will be sold as bait.

135
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:07,740
Of course, when most of us think of fishing bait,

136
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:11,840
we think of another denizen of the dark, night crawlers.

137
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:15,140
And if you're after big, juicy ones, Toronto, Canada,

138
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:18,080
is the worm capital of the world.

139
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:22,000
Pagonus live bait is one of Toronto's biggest names when it comes to worms.

140
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:28,040
Here at Pagonus live bait, we package and distribute 120 million worms annually.

141
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:29,940
And they look like this.

142
00:08:30,820 --> 00:08:33,980
Over a billion worms are exported all across North America.

143
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:37,140
Their scientific name is Lumbricus terrestris.

144
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:45,720
The often cool and damp weather, along with the type of soil around Toronto, are ideal for worms.

145
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:51,420
Pagonas pays local farmers for the right to harvest the nightcrawlers living underground on their land.

146
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:55,340
Dairy farms have the advantage of producing a lot of manure,

147
00:08:55,820 --> 00:08:58,720
which provides nutrients that helps keep worms fat and happy.

148
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:05,880
Once it's dark, vans begin to arrive.

149
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:12,560
Each van driver has delivered a group of pickers from the city to the fields chosen to harvest tonight.

150
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:15,580
This is my own truck. The picker's independent.

151
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:19,520
Whatever they make is written down at the end of the night,

152
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:21,960
and I'm responsible for paying them.

153
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:25,700
Since the van drivers and the pickers aren't on his payroll,

154
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:28,460
Chris Pagonis is never sure how many will show up.

155
00:09:28,940 --> 00:09:31,840
We've had as low as 20, 30, 40 pickers a night,

156
00:09:31,980 --> 00:09:33,600
as high as 200 pickers a night,

157
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:35,700
depending on the quality of the field and the conditions.

158
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:39,500
And being the nightcrawler industry, it is very competitive.

159
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:43,240
As a business, you need excellent worm-producing fields

160
00:09:43,241 --> 00:09:45,460
simply because of the fact that the more worms

161
00:09:45,461 --> 00:09:49,420
you can produce on your farm, the more people you have picking on your farm.

162
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,140
Aside from warm and waterproof clothes, if it's cool and raining like tonight,

163
00:09:57,140 --> 00:10:01,820
standard worm picking equipment includes a 12-volt battery-powered light that's strapped to the

164
00:10:01,821 --> 00:10:03,496
forehead and two coffee cans.

165
00:10:03,497 --> 00:10:07,080
Pickers typically strap them on the sides of their lower legs.

166
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:12,000
One can is for picked worms, and the other contains sawdust they will periodically dab

167
00:10:12,001 --> 00:10:16,000
their fingers in to make sure they haven't gotten too slimy to get a good grip on the worms.

168
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:22,720
Earthworms are called night crawlers because that's what they do to look for food

169
00:10:22,721 --> 00:10:27,840
and possibly a mate. Larger night crawlers can reach lengths of five to six inches

170
00:10:27,841 --> 00:10:29,840
and often don't come up all the way out of the ground.

171
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:36,720
Worms come up head first so basically the art of picking a worm is actually pretty much grabbing

172
00:10:36,721 --> 00:10:39,720
by its head area over here and plucking it out of the ground.

173
00:10:39,721 --> 00:10:43,720
Its natural reaction is to clamp and grip itself with earth.

174
00:10:43,721 --> 00:10:48,720
However, while you're tugging it, it'll loosen itself up.

175
00:10:48,721 --> 00:10:51,720
There's a worm right there.

176
00:10:51,721 --> 00:10:54,720
So by putting a little pressure on it,

177
00:10:54,721 --> 00:10:56,720
this one's a little bit difficult,

178
00:10:56,721 --> 00:10:59,720
you'll pull it right out of the ground.

179
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:04,720
Pickers spend most of the night crouched or bent over.

180
00:11:04,721 --> 00:11:07,760
They can be wet, cold, and must move quickly and quietly

181
00:11:07,761 --> 00:11:10,080
or the worms will dart back onto the surface.

182
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:13,500
But there's a simple reason why they're willing to spend a sleepless night

183
00:11:13,501 --> 00:11:16,300
traversing a field full of mud, manure, and worms.

184
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:21,860
Pickers generally earn anywhere between $18 and $25 a thousand.

185
00:11:22,340 --> 00:11:26,500
And on average, picker can pick 15 to 20, 25,000 worms on a given night.

186
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:27,900
However, that's not every night.

187
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:31,980
Sometimes they work 8 hours or 10 hours and they only pick 1,000 worms.

188
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:33,120
Sometimes they don't pick anything.

189
00:11:33,121 --> 00:11:35,860
but on a super night they can pick up to 30,000 worms.

190
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:40,100
The next morning, one by one,

191
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:42,920
the vans arrive at the Pagonis facility

192
00:11:42,921 --> 00:11:45,000
to drop off their portion of the night's catch.

193
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,360
I finally arrived back here in Toronto

194
00:11:49,361 --> 00:11:50,820
after dropping off all the pickers.

195
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,860
I have about 200,000 worms in the back

196
00:11:52,861 --> 00:11:54,720
that I have to unload, and then my job is done.

197
00:11:57,300 --> 00:12:00,160
Upon arrival, workers place about 500 worms

198
00:12:00,161 --> 00:12:01,740
into containers filled with soil

199
00:12:01,741 --> 00:12:05,040
containing organic matter that provides nutrition for the worms.

200
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:10,140
Then, to keep them fresher longer, it's off to the cooler.

201
00:12:13,020 --> 00:12:14,720
Once the worms are on the pallet,

202
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:17,920
we bring them into our refrigerated storage area.

203
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:20,960
The worms are kept at about 35 degrees Fahrenheit

204
00:12:20,961 --> 00:12:23,640
or 2 degrees Celsius, and the lights are kept on as well

205
00:12:23,641 --> 00:12:25,260
to keep the worms inside the boxes.

206
00:12:25,460 --> 00:12:27,220
They're sensitive to the light. They don't like it.

207
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:29,820
Each cooler holds about 80 skid spots.

208
00:12:29,821 --> 00:12:32,460
Each skid holds about 50,000 worms.

209
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:36,400
So this cooler is holding about 4 million worms at this moment.

210
00:12:37,920 --> 00:12:39,760
There are four coolers in the facility,

211
00:12:40,140 --> 00:12:41,960
and the worms can stay there for months.

212
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:45,340
To fill wholesale orders, big batches of worms

213
00:12:45,341 --> 00:12:46,360
are prepared for shipment.

214
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:49,460
Ampagonis also supplies directly to retailers.

215
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:54,040
They've also recently automated one packaging line.

216
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:58,500
This is our retail packaging area

217
00:12:58,501 --> 00:13:00,200
where we take worms that have been taken out

218
00:13:00,201 --> 00:13:01,800
of their original bedding.

219
00:13:01,801 --> 00:13:05,240
And what we do then is we place 12, 18, or 24 worms

220
00:13:05,241 --> 00:13:07,640
in each container.

221
00:13:07,641 --> 00:13:09,680
Once we pack our worms, our worms are then

222
00:13:09,681 --> 00:13:13,840
ready to ship all over North America.

223
00:13:13,841 --> 00:13:18,260
Few nocturnal rituals are as benign as this one.

224
00:13:18,261 --> 00:13:20,380
Life and death hang in the balance,

225
00:13:20,381 --> 00:13:23,100
as the modern soldier relies on high technology

226
00:13:23,101 --> 00:13:26,060
to peel away the darkness.

227
00:13:26,061 --> 00:13:28,360
And far above him, pilots would be flying

228
00:13:28,361 --> 00:13:33,160
without the innovation that turns night into day.

229
00:13:33,260 --> 00:13:36,860
While foot-long night crawlers can be found in North America,

230
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:39,900
one specimen of a South African giant earthworm

231
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,400
measured a record-shattering 22 feet.

232
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:45,920
Night will return on Modern Marvels.

233
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:52,120
The night is an arena of intrigue,

234
00:13:52,220 --> 00:13:55,820
where danger or even death may be lurking in the darkness.

235
00:13:55,821 --> 00:13:59,320
Nature has given owls a strategic nocturnal advantage

236
00:13:59,321 --> 00:14:03,820
by equipping them with large pupils that peer into the night far better than we can.

237
00:14:03,821 --> 00:14:08,820
But modern technology has given us a similar ability.

238
00:14:14,820 --> 00:14:19,820
And nowhere is this more a matter of survival than in warfare.

239
00:14:19,821 --> 00:14:24,820
Nightline, a company located in Mountain City, Tennessee,

240
00:14:24,821 --> 00:14:27,260
specializes in night vision equipment

241
00:14:27,261 --> 00:14:30,480
and is owned and operated by former military members

242
00:14:30,481 --> 00:14:33,200
trained in night operations and counterinsurgency.

243
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:38,520
What night vision does is give the user

244
00:14:38,521 --> 00:14:40,940
a heightened sense of security

245
00:14:40,941 --> 00:14:43,820
and advantage over an enemy that does not have it.

246
00:14:44,460 --> 00:14:47,820
It allows his movement to be stealthier and more deadly.

247
00:14:48,340 --> 00:14:51,280
A team of six men can take on a company of 200.

248
00:14:51,281 --> 00:14:55,280
Here at Nightline, we manufacture a lot of goggles, weapon

249
00:14:55,281 --> 00:14:58,280
sights, and other night vision devices, thousands per year,

250
00:14:58,281 --> 00:15:00,280
that can go to the US Army, Homeland Security, and other

251
00:15:00,281 --> 00:15:02,280
NATO-friendly countries.

252
00:15:02,281 --> 00:15:06,280
Night vision equipment was first used during the Vietnam War,

253
00:15:06,281 --> 00:15:10,280
but since then, it's become smaller and more light-sensitive.

254
00:15:10,281 --> 00:15:14,280
The green screen images typically associated with night vision

255
00:15:14,281 --> 00:15:18,280
are produced through a process called image intensification.

256
00:15:18,281 --> 00:15:23,280
In every night sky, there's some amount of ambient light.

257
00:15:23,281 --> 00:15:25,280
It may come from street lights.

258
00:15:25,281 --> 00:15:28,280
It may come from a city 100 miles away.

259
00:15:28,281 --> 00:15:31,280
It may come from the moon or stars.

260
00:15:31,281 --> 00:15:33,280
Even in a cloud-covered night,

261
00:15:33,281 --> 00:15:35,280
there's a certain amount of ambient light.

262
00:15:35,281 --> 00:15:37,280
What little available light there is

263
00:15:37,281 --> 00:15:39,280
enters the front of a night vision device

264
00:15:39,281 --> 00:15:42,280
through the objective lens.

265
00:15:42,281 --> 00:15:44,280
The lens captures the ambient light

266
00:15:44,281 --> 00:15:47,280
and directs it to an image intensifier tube.

267
00:15:47,281 --> 00:15:51,280
First, the photons of light energy are converted into electrons.

268
00:15:51,281 --> 00:15:56,280
Then as each electron passes through one of the tiny holes in the microchannel plate,

269
00:15:56,281 --> 00:16:00,280
it's multiplied into thousands of additional electrons.

270
00:16:00,281 --> 00:16:06,280
At the end of the image intensifier tube, the electrons hit a screen coated with phosphors,

271
00:16:06,281 --> 00:16:10,280
creating the enhanced image seen in the eyepiece.

272
00:16:10,281 --> 00:16:18,240
The phosphor screen is green because green is the easiest color for the eye to deal with and absorb.

273
00:16:18,740 --> 00:16:25,200
But there are tubes made in black and white, and there are tubes made in blue for special purposes.

274
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:29,480
To ensure that its night vision gear is used properly in the field,

275
00:16:30,840 --> 00:16:34,520
Nightline offers training courses to military and law enforcement personnel.

276
00:16:35,320 --> 00:16:40,240
These are the monocular night vision devices that we are going to be using this evening.

277
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:45,280
These are designed for either helmet-mounted or head-mounted systems.

278
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:53,280
A computer-generated virtual firing range allows the trainees to become adept at using the equipment.

279
00:16:58,280 --> 00:17:00,280
Then it's time to get used to the dark.

280
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:05,280
Now we're in the backfire facility where we have our light-controlled scenario rooms.

281
00:17:05,281 --> 00:17:06,060
scenario rooms.

282
00:17:06,061 --> 00:17:10,220
The first room is the night vision control room where we can control the lights,

283
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:14,620
where they can get used to the night vision gear under light levels under various conditions.

284
00:17:15,560 --> 00:17:19,980
In the next huge room, Nightline alters the scenarios depending on the trainees.

285
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:26,000
Today, because local sheriff's deputies are involved, the setting is a school that has

286
00:17:26,001 --> 00:17:27,455
been overrun by gunmen.

287
00:17:27,456 --> 00:17:31,820
Normally, the trainees would enter the room for the first time in the

288
00:17:31,821 --> 00:17:34,820
But just for modern marvels, they first move through the course

289
00:17:34,821 --> 00:17:37,820
with the lights on, so we can see what they're up against.

290
00:17:37,821 --> 00:17:44,820
Now we'll attach a night vision lens to our high-definition camera

291
00:17:44,821 --> 00:17:48,820
that uses the same type of image intensification technology.

292
00:17:48,821 --> 00:17:52,820
See for yourself what it's like in the middle of the action

293
00:17:52,821 --> 00:17:54,820
with the lights off.

294
00:17:54,821 --> 00:17:57,820
The trainee's instructor, who was on the ground,

295
00:17:57,821 --> 00:18:04,620
The trainees instructor, who is also wearing night vision equipment, keeps track of their

296
00:18:04,621 --> 00:18:08,060
progress from the catwalks above.

297
00:18:08,061 --> 00:18:17,180
Nightline staff also assembles and offers training in another type of night vision,

298
00:18:17,181 --> 00:18:18,500
thermal imaging.

299
00:18:18,501 --> 00:18:24,000
Instead of amplifying light, this technology detects and sees heat.

300
00:18:24,001 --> 00:18:25,760
We are here on a very dark night.

301
00:18:25,761 --> 00:18:30,680
I can only see the area right in front of me due to the lights, but anything beyond the

302
00:18:30,681 --> 00:18:33,160
lights cannot be seen.

303
00:18:33,161 --> 00:18:38,260
But with the thermal imager, if I look over here, I can see some vehicles, and I can tell

304
00:18:38,261 --> 00:18:41,640
which engines have been run recently because of the heat.

305
00:18:41,641 --> 00:18:47,120
And if I look over here, I can see some people along the edge of the cornfield and out near

306
00:18:47,121 --> 00:18:48,120
the river.

307
00:18:48,121 --> 00:18:52,640
Everything that's warm-blooded or has some sort of heat associated with it can be seen

308
00:18:52,641 --> 00:18:55,520
by a thermal imaging device.

309
00:18:55,521 --> 00:18:59,640
A lens captures the varied amounts of infrared light emitted by the objects in view.

310
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:03,700
This light is scanned by an array of infrared detector elements

311
00:19:03,701 --> 00:19:07,780
that creates a very detailed temperature pattern called a thermogram.

312
00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:11,820
The pattern is translated into electric impulses.

313
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:17,180
Then a signal processing unit converts it into the display in the eyepiece.

314
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:23,140
Thermal imaging devices can sense temperatures ranging from minus 4

315
00:19:23,141 --> 00:19:25,680
to 3,600 degrees Fahrenheit

316
00:19:25,681 --> 00:19:27,760
and detect changes in temperature

317
00:19:27,761 --> 00:19:29,460
as little as about a half of a degree.

318
00:19:31,540 --> 00:19:34,720
As the military uses the most sophisticated night vision

319
00:19:34,721 --> 00:19:35,421
on the ground,

320
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:39,640
what about civilian pilots

321
00:19:39,641 --> 00:19:42,480
barreling through the night sky at nearly Mach 1?

322
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:45,940
At the Rockwell Collins Advanced Technology Center

323
00:19:45,941 --> 00:19:47,060
in Cedar Rapids, Iowa,

324
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:50,060
a new flight deck system called Synthetic Vision

325
00:19:50,061 --> 00:19:52,360
is in the final stages of development.

326
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:58,140
Instead of a variety of traditional aircraft instruments to assess position,

327
00:19:58,141 --> 00:20:03,140
a console display provides pilots with a visual representation of the terrain and other hazards.

328
00:20:03,141 --> 00:20:09,140
Synthetic vision is a virtual world that was developed from the database,

329
00:20:09,141 --> 00:20:15,140
and it's a view into the database and using GPS technology that gives me my position information,

330
00:20:15,141 --> 00:20:19,140
I can generate a synthetic view of what is outside the airplane.

331
00:20:19,141 --> 00:20:24,140
Right now we're flying with daytime visual setup in good meteorological conditions,

332
00:20:24,141 --> 00:20:28,140
so we don't have any issues with regard to the need for synthetic vision.

333
00:20:28,141 --> 00:20:36,140
But if we select the night mode, then we can see an enhancement through the synthetic vision on the heads-down display.

334
00:20:36,141 --> 00:20:40,140
The airspeed indicator on the left side showing our current airspeed.

335
00:20:40,141 --> 00:20:45,140
The display in the center is our radio altimeter showing height above the terrain.

336
00:20:45,141 --> 00:20:50,560
And on the right side is the height above sea level in barometric altitude.

337
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:55,100
And the red terrain ahead is representing a hazard that we need to avoid.

338
00:20:55,560 --> 00:20:58,300
So we bring our flight path vector up above that and clear the terrain.

339
00:21:01,100 --> 00:21:06,760
The representational terrain is drawn from an assemblage of data gathered from millions of flight hours,

340
00:21:07,040 --> 00:21:09,120
logged on more than 20,000 aircraft.

341
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:14,900
Along with a massive amount of data gathered from the space shuttle mapping missions,

342
00:21:15,140 --> 00:21:19,140
The video game-like visuals were intentionally simplified.

343
00:21:19,141 --> 00:21:21,140
In the development of the displays,

344
00:21:21,141 --> 00:21:24,140
we brought pilots in and looked at different displays.

345
00:21:24,141 --> 00:21:27,140
We found that when it was photorealistic,

346
00:21:27,141 --> 00:21:30,140
pilots took longer to see exactly what was going on

347
00:21:30,141 --> 00:21:33,140
with the display than if we had a less cluttered view of the world.

348
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:39,140
Synthetic vision has undergone exhaustive in-flight trials

349
00:21:39,141 --> 00:21:43,140
on a modified version of Bombardier's Challenger 300 business jet.

350
00:21:45,140 --> 00:21:49,220
since that company's aircraft will be the first to incorporate the system.

351
00:21:51,240 --> 00:21:54,860
We joined the crew as they took off from Cedar Rapids for another run.

352
00:21:57,340 --> 00:22:02,060
Once the sun sets, the terrain disappears outside the cockpit,

353
00:22:02,520 --> 00:22:05,200
but the sun never sets on the synthetic vision screen,

354
00:22:05,980 --> 00:22:09,260
even over the 14,000-foot peaks of the Rocky Mountains.

355
00:22:10,140 --> 00:22:13,220
Okay, the synthetic vision display that we have up here

356
00:22:13,221 --> 00:22:15,160
is quite helpful for the pilot.

357
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:19,260
We're at 17,000, and our flight path vector is right here.

358
00:22:19,460 --> 00:22:23,140
This is showing us that we're completely clearing any of the terrain.

359
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:25,660
Since this is a test aircraft,

360
00:22:26,180 --> 00:22:29,400
the synthetic vision equipment isn't built into the plane's avionics.

361
00:22:30,280 --> 00:22:34,140
Instead, it's housed in the flight test engineer's equipment console in the cabin.

362
00:22:35,180 --> 00:22:39,500
I'm making sure that the PCs are generating the correct synthetic image,

363
00:22:39,760 --> 00:22:42,380
and that's exactly the same image the pilots are seeing up the front.

364
00:22:42,381 --> 00:22:48,060
We're using very accurate GPS, part of the wide area augmentation system, to position

365
00:22:48,061 --> 00:22:49,517
the airplane in real time.

366
00:22:49,518 --> 00:22:53,160
And the airplane's position is being updated five times a second,

367
00:22:53,161 --> 00:22:56,380
and we can position ourselves to an accuracy of less than 30 feet.

368
00:22:56,381 --> 00:23:01,500
Aside from the geographical terrain, synthetic vision data also displays any

369
00:23:01,501 --> 00:23:06,814
obstacles over 200 feet, including radio towers and tall buildings.

370
00:23:06,815 --> 00:23:08,480
In addition, it helps

371
00:23:08,481 --> 00:23:10,380
the pilots locate airports.

372
00:23:10,381 --> 00:23:16,500
We've turned down the valley now and this dome is over the airport itself and

373
00:23:16,501 --> 00:23:22,420
that enhances the visibility of where the landing field is. As we approach the

374
00:23:22,421 --> 00:23:30,360
dome fades away and reveals the landing runway. Many proclaim this innovative

375
00:23:30,361 --> 00:23:34,940
navigational system the cockpit of the future.

376
00:23:34,941 --> 00:23:42,780
We've taken that final last quantum step that enables the pilot to visualize exactly

377
00:23:42,781 --> 00:23:47,340
where he is in space and where he is with respect to the ground.

378
00:23:47,341 --> 00:23:52,960
From above, the lights below can make cities look deceptively peaceful.

379
00:23:52,961 --> 00:23:56,820
But trouble often brews in the dark corners of the urban jungle.

380
00:23:56,821 --> 00:24:03,140
Luckily for us, cops have the technology to scorch the night and take a bite out of crime.

